{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices (often by ~1–3 EUR early, widening to ~5–12 EUR at longer horizons during sell-offs). Apply a downward level shift to the entire path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when recent history shows a clear decline, the model projects stabilization/sideways too quickly. Add extra negative drift proportional to the last 10–20 day slope; strongest correction in the first 1–2 weeks ahead.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: forecast error increases with lead time (especially beyond ~day 10). Use a horizon ramp correction (small adjustment days 1–5, moderate days 6–15, large days 16–30).",
    "Over-optimistic rebounds: the model frequently predicts rebounds to mid-70s while outcomes continue falling into low/mid-60s. Suppress rebound amplitude after a drawdown; cap any 2–4 week bounce to a fraction of the last 2-week drop (e.g., ≤30–40%).",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: model mean-reverts to a level above the true regime (new lower equilibrium). Re-anchor mean reversion to a lower reference level based on the recent 20–30 day median/EMA rather than older higher prices.",
    "Volatility asymmetry: model captures small day-to-day moves but misses larger downside tails. Add a downside skew adjustment when recent volatility is elevated and trend is negative (increase probability/size of further declines)."
  ]
}
